WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.680
What is a perceptron? It's a 1958 idea that sits at the heart of modern AI.

2
00:00:07.101 --> 00:00:10.641
It starts with an input. Here, a tiny four-by-four picture.

3
00:00:10.793 --> 00:00:13.693
Each pixel is either on, a one, or off, a zero.

4
00:00:15.528 --> 00:00:18.498
Every pixel gets a weight, set by its own knob.

5
00:00:18.644 --> 00:00:23.424
Positive weights push the answer towards X. Negative weights push it towards O.

6
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:27.807
The perceptron adds up the weights of the pixels that are lit.

7
00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:32.147
The total is plus twenty. Positive, so it answers X.

8
00:00:33.426 --> 00:00:36.546
So how does it learn? We start with every knob at zero.

9
00:00:36.691 --> 00:00:40.701
Show it an X. It gets it wrong, so we nudge the lit knobs towards X.

10
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.890
Now an O. Wrong again, so we nudge them towards O.

11
00:00:45.209 --> 00:00:47.369
Try again, and now both are right.

12
00:00:50.036 --> 00:00:51.146
It's learned!

13
00:00:53.064 --> 00:00:59.104
But a single perceptron can only draw one straight line, so it can't solve problems like this one, called XOR.

14
00:00:59.252 --> 00:01:04.202
Stack lots of them in layers, though, and you get the neural networks behind modern AI.

15
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.233
Try it yourself, with Perceptron Bench.
